# SYNOPSIS

```usage
trace -c CATEGORIES [-t DURATION] [-o FILE]
trace -p [FILE]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`trace` is the kernel's trace (ADR-0049, 20 §7) from the shell, through procfs(4)'s `/proc/trace`, which only an administrator sees.

With `-c` it starts a trace of the comma-separated CATEGORIES (`sched`, `ipc`, `irq`, `vm`, `futex`, `syscall`, `mark`), waits DURATION (digits then `ms`, `s` or `m`; a second by default), stops it, and saves the events, 32-byte records ordered by time, to FILE (`trace.out` by default), saying how many.

With `-p` it prints the events of FILE, or the live `/proc/trace/events` without one, one ndb record a line: `time`, `cpu`, the `task` and `thread` that wrote it, `kind`, and the kind's two words (a mark's text; a switch's, wake's or donation's threads by task and thread).

# EXAMPLES

```
trace -c sched,ipc -t 2s -o /tmp/boot.trace
trace -p /tmp/boot.trace | grep kind=wake
```

